\details{
The function can read:
\itemize{
\item one or more individual \code{.zip} files,
\item one or more directories containing \code{.zip} files,
\item optionally only files matching selected years.
}

Small zip files can be excluded using \code{min_file_size}, as unusually small
files are often incomplete or corrupted and may fail in the underlying
DATRAS reader functions.

DATRAS zip archives are typically much larger than a few kilobytes, so very
small files are often suspicious and may represent failed downloads or
damaged archives.

Reading a large number of DATRAS files into R can require substantial memory,
especially when combining multiple surveys or many years. The following
options can substantially reduce peak memory use:

\itemize{
\item \code{drop_hl = TRUE} drops the length-frequency table (\code{HL}) immediately
after each file is read. \code{HL} is typically the largest table and can be
omitted when only haul-level metadata is needed.
\item \code{drop_ca = TRUE} drops the biological sampling table (\code{CA}) in the
same way.
\item \code{prune = TRUE} trims all three tables to a compact set of core
columns. Can be combined with \code{drop_hl} / \code{drop_ca}.
}

When loading a very large database (many surveys, many years) in a single
call still exceeds available memory even after using the options above,
consider loading in parts and combining with \code{\link[=c]{c()}}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{x1 <- read_datras("~/data/DATRAS", surveys = c("NS-IBTS", "BITS"),
                  drop_ca = TRUE)
x2 <- read_datras("~/data/DATRAS", surveys = c("EVHOE", "IBTS-MED"),
                  drop_ca = TRUE)
x_all <- c(x1, x2)
rm(x1, x2)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

If you need a different set of retained columns than provided by
\code{\link[=prune_datras]{prune_datras()}}, you may wish to apply your own pruning function after
reading or adapt the pruning code.

Records in the \code{CA} table frequently lack the station and haul numbers that
make up \code{haul.id}, and are matched back to a haul by survey, year, quarter,
country, ship, and statistical rectangle. When that match is not unique, the
\code{strict} argument decides what happens: \code{strict = FALSE} picks
one of the candidate hauls at random, whereas the default \code{strict = TRUE} leaves the
record unmatched. The number of records affected can be checked afterwards
with \code{sum(is.na(x[["CA"]]$haul.id))}. Note that
\code{\link[=download_datras]{download_datras()}} uses the equivalent of \code{strict = TRUE} when downloading
